# Hosting and origin plan

Status: recommended release plan; no infrastructure or auth settings changed.

`brief.md` remains the authority for owner decisions. This plan preserves short app addresses, the signed-in product origin, shared production authentication, and invited-only apps. `hosting.md` describes the current implementation, not a safe target.

## Release decision

Do not open the full app-hosting beta next week on the current layout. Arbitrary user HTML and JavaScript must stop executing with a `plinth.example` origin before external apps are enabled. The freeze forbids the new domain, DNS, and certificates needed by the target below. Schedule the full beta after the freeze and after the release checks pass; the passage of two months alone is not sufficient.

During the freeze, retain the signed-in product and short addresses, but serve only Plinth-owned unavailable/coming-soon pages at app addresses. Keep uploading and name reservation available if those existing flows can operate without executing uploads. Disable execution in editor previews too. No uploaded HTML, SVG documents, JavaScript, or other active content may be served inline on any product host or alternate storage/provider URL under the product's authority. Download-only access, if retained, must use attachment disposition and `nosniff`, without inline preview.

This deliberately changes the beta date rather than silently dropping native browser storage, private apps, or short addresses. A freeze exception could accelerate the target, but this plan does not assume one. No owner reply is needed to complete this document; domain provisioning and launch are later operational work.

## What is wrong with today's layout

- `name.plinth.example` and `app.plinth.example` are different **origins**, so they normally have separate DOM access and `localStorage`. They are nevertheless the same **site**, and a cookie scoped to `plinth.example` is sent to both. Moving uploads to another subdomain, including `content.plinth.example`, does not establish the needed cookie/site boundary. See [MDN cookies](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Cookies) and [site definition](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Site).
- If a shared session cookie is script-readable, an uploaded app can read and exfiltrate it. `HttpOnly` prevents that read, but scripts can still initiate credentialed requests. Same-site requests do not gain a cross-site defense from `SameSite`. CORS can restrict response reads; it does not generally prevent requests or CSRF. Tenant JavaScript can also set parent-domain cookies, creating cookie-injection and session-confusion risks. We have not inspected the actual provider cookie flags or CSRF protections and do not assert a demonstrated account takeover.
- The edge seeing the cookie establishes neither invitation membership nor safe execution. Private delivery needs a verified identity **and** current permission for the particular app, on every protected file request.

## Target layout after the freeze

`plinthusercontent.example` below is a proposed, separate registrable domain used to specify the architecture. It is not an existing resource or a purchased name. Confirm ownership, availability, and the real registrable-domain boundary before provisioning its replacement.

| Address | What runs there | Authentication and state |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `https://plinth.example` | Plinth-owned public content, if present | Never uploaded active content |
| `https://app.plinth.example` | Signed-in editor, invitations, account and publishing UI | Existing managed production auth; no uploaded scripts in its document |
| `https://<name>.plinth.example/<path>` | Plinth-owned app wrapper and access UI | Shared managed session; validates invitation before private launch |
| `https://<app-id>.plinthusercontent.example/<path>` | That app's uploaded HTML, CSS, JS and assets | No Plinth account cookie or auth SDK; stable app origin and its own browser storage |

Keep the short address in the location bar by embedding the content origin in the trusted wrapper. Do not redirect the default visit to a longer content URL. Map the short name to an immutable app ID in the database; the content hostname remains stable across deploys and name changes. Never reuse a content hostname for a different app or owner. Do not place multiple mutually untrusted apps at paths on one origin.

Only trusted Plinth bytes may be returned from **any path** on a product host. This includes error pages, fallback routes, attachments, metadata and previews. Escape app names and other metadata. Uploaded assets belong on the content domain even when they look harmless. Buckets stay private behind the delivery edge; the database owns host mapping, deployed version, visibility and permissions, while object storage owns file bytes.

Use separate control and content routing policies and credentials. The content delivery edge gets read-only access to the required published objects and narrow access to permission checks; it must not expose control-plane credentials to uploaded code. Continue using the existing edge and object store where suitable. No hosting-provider migration is required by this plan.

## Wrapper, execution and browser storage

The wrapper embeds only the exact content hostname derived from the database. Start with `sandbox="allow-scripts allow-same-origin"`: JavaScript and native origin storage work while parent navigation, popups, forms and sensitive browser capabilities remain restricted by default. Additional capabilities require an explicit supported-app contract and browser checks. The combination is acceptable here only because the frame is on a separate, untrusted content origin; never use it for an uploaded frame on the wrapper's own origin. See [MDN iframe sandbox guidance](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/iframe).

Serve an enforced CSP on uploaded documents as well as a frame sandbox so visiting the content URL directly cannot bypass the intended restrictions. The wrapper CSP admits its own reviewed scripts and only that app's frame origin. Do not copy the wrapper's script policy onto arbitrary apps and assume compatibility. Deny camera, microphone and other privileged features unless explicitly supported. Prevent uploaded service-worker registration for the initial release, including edge rejection of worker-script requests, and test that this holds. A worker must not become a stale authorization or routing layer.

Use `postMessage` only for a small, validated protocol such as ready/error and optional navigation synchronization. Check both the exact `event.origin` and frame `event.source`; send to an exact target origin. Never relay generic authenticated fetches or expose the account session. Deep links and relative assets must resolve on the content origin; the wrapper maps requested paths and queries without interpreting them as external URLs. Hash routing, back/forward, and reload are acceptance checks, not assumed iframe behavior.

Native `localStorage` remains in the app's stable content origin. A new deployment must not generate a new hostname. It is browser-local state, not an encrypted vault or an invitation boundary: scripts deployed by the app owner can read that app's stored data. Warn people against entering secrets into an app they do not trust.

Cross-site embedding introduces storage partitioning and browser privacy restrictions. The wrapper's top-level site is stable, but that does not guarantee persistent storage on every browser. Verify write/reload/browser-restart behavior and private browsing behavior in the supported browsers before promising persistence. Direct content visits may have a different storage partition from embedded visits. Do not offer a silent top-level fallback that appears to lose data. If the supported-browser tests fail, keep the launch blocked and revisit the product contract explicitly; do not quietly replace `localStorage` with a different API. See [MDN localStorage](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/localStorage) and [third-party storage restrictions](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Privacy/Guides/Third-party_cookies).

## Private app access

1. The trusted wrapper sends signed-out visitors through the existing managed auth flow on `app.plinth.example`. Carry the destination as a server-validated app ID and relative path, bound to the login transaction. Never accept an arbitrary redirect host. No new OAuth callback host is required.
2. The server verifies the provider session using the provider's supported verifier, then checks current invited-user membership for that app. Being signed in, knowing the hostname, or owning a different app grants nothing.
3. Issue a random, single-use launch code bound to the user, app ID, exact content host and current permission version. Proposed lifetime: 60 seconds. Give it to a Plinth-owned bootstrap at the content origin through a fragment, clear the fragment before uploaded code runs, and redeem it through a POST. Do not put account tokens or reusable grants in query strings, asset URLs, logs or referrers.
4. Redemption rechecks permission and creates an app-specific delivery session. Use a host-only `__Host-` cookie with `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `Path=/`, no `Domain`, and a proposed maximum lifetime of five minutes. Embedded delivery requires `SameSite=None` and a browser-supported partitioned-cookie strategy. This is a content-access session, never a Plinth account session. See [MDN cookie attributes and prefixes](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Set-Cookie).
5. The content edge validates the delivery session and current permission on every HTML, JS, CSS, image, range and conditional request, including direct content URLs. Revocation or deletion denies subsequent requests immediately; fail closed if permission cannot be established. Sign-out invalidates related delivery sessions. Expiry can be renewed through the trusted wrapper only after reauthorization.

Embedded cookies can be blocked even when attributes look correct. Demonstrate this entire flow on the supported browser matrix with third-party cookies blocked before shipping private apps. Do not work around failure by exposing private object URLs, accepting the account cookie on the content domain, or making an opaque hostname the access control. The exact cookie transport remains an implementation gate, not a claim of browser compatibility.

The delivery session authorizes reading one app's published files; assume that app's code can cause reads within that scope. It cannot call account APIs or issue grants for another app. Private delivery prevents unauthorized distribution by the service; it cannot stop an authorized viewer or the app author from copying content, or erase bytes already downloaded.

## HTTP and edge rules

- Reserve product and infrastructure hostnames before tenant lookup, including `app`, auth-provider hosts and existing operational names. Match normalized hosts exactly; reject unknown hosts and untrusted forwarded-host values. Never let a tenant shadow a product route or resolve an object key outside its published manifest. Normalize paths and reject traversal and encoded traversal.
- On control APIs, require exact trusted origins for credentialed browser access and CSRF protection on mutations; no wildcard subdomain allowlist and no content-origin allowlist. Reject `null` browser origins for account operations. Origin checks complement session authorization; they do not authenticate non-browser clients. Never mutate state through GET.
- Ignore uploaded response headers and redirects. The edge owns MIME types, `nosniff`, CSP, cache policy and cookies. Block legacy `document.domain` relaxation on trusted surfaces. Use `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` for launch and private content responses. Do not log cookies, launch codes or private paths unnecessarily.
- Private responses, bootstrap exchanges, denials and redirects use `Cache-Control: private, no-store`, with shared-cache bypass. Authorize before any object-cache hit, conditional `304`, or range response. Buckets and provider aliases must not bypass that gate.
- Public immutable assets may use shared caching keyed by content host, deployment version and normalized path. Public HTML has bounded caching and explicit purge on publication or visibility change. Changing an app to private must purge public caches and disable old public delivery routes before acknowledging the change. Previously downloaded public bytes cannot be recalled.
- Bound upload size, file count, expanded archive size and path processing; rate-limit publishing, launches and delivery. Include per-app disable and a global execution kill switch. Rejected hosts and denied private files return content-free errors without invitation or account details.

## Why the freeze has no equivalent workaround

| Candidate | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| Keep user HTML top-level on short hosts and add CSP/CORS | Reject: policies do not turn arbitrary same-site scripts into a separate cookie boundary. A policy that prohibits all uploaded JS changes the product. |
| Move execution to another `plinth.example` subdomain | Reject: different origin, same parent cookie scope and site. |
| Opaque sandbox, such as `sandbox="allow-scripts"`, including `srcdoc` | Can isolate script authority, but disables native origin storage. Not the promised app runtime. Sandbox does not itself strip cookies from every network request. |
| Add `allow-same-origin` to that sandbox on a product host | Reject: restores the origin and its cookie scope; with same-origin scripts it can also undermine the embedding sandbox. |
| Shim `localStorage` through messages to the wrapper | Reject for this beta: synchronous native storage semantics cannot be replaced transparently by an asynchronous bridge. It adds a new data and security contract. |
| Make auth host-only or add the product domain to the Public Suffix List | Not an authorized substitute: conflicts with the decided shared-session model and needs a separate provider/session migration. PSL changes are not an instant beta fix. |
| Use an assumed provider domain or another existing domain | No such isolated resource is documented. If one is later proven to exist with suitable routing, TLS and isolation, reassess the schedule against the same release gates. |

## Delivery order and release evidence

1. Before opening access, implement and verify the freeze-time execution block across short hosts, editor previews, raw-file routes and aliases. If any external apps already run, treat containment and an exposure assessment as immediate work; inspect actual cookie flags and request protections before deciding whether session invalidation is needed. Do not claim that cookies were stolen without evidence.
2. Develop and test the split locally with distinct test sites and non-production credentials. Different localhost ports test origins, but do not prove cross-site cookie behavior. During the freeze, use only already-provisioned staging resources; if none can model the boundary, record live browser checks as blocked rather than creating new DNS or certificates.
3. After the freeze, provision the separate content domain, wildcard DNS/TLS and stage-isolated content routing. Inventory existing product DNS and certificates first. Document concrete domains, provider projects, allowed origins, environment contracts and operational ownership alongside the implementation. Keep staging content and auth separate from production. Centralize origin construction and reject stage/host mismatches.
4. Implement the trusted wrapper, content edge, launch redemption and revocation, then migrate routing without ever falling back to the old uploaded top-level document. Use a server-side switch so rollback produces an unavailable page rather than unsafe execution.
5. Exercise malicious app A against the wrapper, account APIs and private app B: cookie reads/writes, forged messages, CSRF, arbitrary redirects, direct files, object aliases and poisoned caches. Verify that no platform credential reaches app code, storage, URLs or logs. Verify invited, uninvited, signed-out, expired and revoked access, including warmed caches and direct deep links.
6. Test native storage isolation between apps and persistence across reloads, redeploys and name changes in Chromium, Firefox and Safari, including mobile Safari and privacy restrictions. Test assets, module imports, forms if enabled, hash/deep links, back/forward and blocked frame escape. Confirm unsupported features fail with clear product-owned messages.
7. Open the beta only after those checks pass on the actual deployed layout. Record evidence and remaining limitations. Infrastructure setup, browser tests and launch are pending; this documentation review did not execute them.

Changing origins will not carry existing `localStorage` across automatically. Assume there is no external beta data yet. If that is false, first inventory affected users and provide a deliberate export/import path. Do not run legacy uploaded scripts to migrate data or copy arbitrary storage into the signed-in product. Also retire any legacy service-worker registrations and caches before reusing old hosts for trusted wrappers; where that cannot be guaranteed, keep those hosts unavailable until a safe retirement is verified.

## Assumptions and owner follow-up

- Uploads are untrusted arbitrary static web apps, including AI-written code. Author reputation and invitation-only distribution do not establish code trust. Server-side user code execution is outside this plan.
- Short addresses should remain visible while an app runs. The wrapper preserves this stronger interpretation of the brief; it is not merely a short redirect service.
- Native `localStorage` is a compatibility requirement because the brief identifies it as a common app dependency. No promise is made that all browser APIs or arbitrary top-level-only apps work in an iframe.
- The shared production auth cookie is a settled constraint. We have no provider name, cookie inventory, DNS inventory, existing isolated content domain, supported-browser policy or implementation evidence beyond the two supplied docs. Proposed lifetimes and the browser matrix above are conservative planning defaults.
- The infrastructure freeze is binding and includes the prerequisites for the content domain. A delayed full beta is preferable to claiming an equivalent secure runtime that the known resources cannot provide. The owner should confirm the revised launch date and eventual content-domain name on return; neither is silently provisioned or changed here.

The next implementation task is the freeze-time execution block. The next infrastructure task, once allowed, is the isolated content domain. The full beta remains gated on both origin isolation and demonstrated private delivery and storage behavior.
